{"tool_name":"nssnpanalyzer","contents":["oeb","bioschemas","biotools"],"fetched_metadata":{"biotools":{"id":"nssnpanalyzer","home":"http://snpanalyzer.uthsc.edu/","summary":"nsSNPAnalyzer is a tool to predict whether a nonsynonymous single nucleotide polymorphism (nsSNP) is phenotypically neutral or disease associated.","addition_date":"2017-02-10T11:35:31Z","last_update_date":"2024-11-24T20:28:01.237767Z","tool_type":["Web application"],"biotools_curie":"biotools:nssnpanalyzer","credit":[{"email":"ycui2@uthsc.edu","name":"nsSNPAnalyzer Support","typeEntity":"Person","typeRole":["Primary contact"]}],"documentation":[{"type":["General"],"url":"http://snpanalyzer.uthsc.edu/help.htm"}],"edit_permission":{"type":"private"},"function":[{"operation":[{"term":"Haplotype mapping","uri":"http://edamontology.org/operation_0487"},{"term":"PCR primer design","uri":"http://edamontology.org/operation_0308"},{"term":"SNP annotation","uri":"http://edamontology.org/operation_3661"},{"term":"SNP detection","uri":"http://edamontology.org/operation_0484"}]}],"name":"nsSNPAnalyzer","operating_system":["Linux","Mac","Windows"],"owner":"ELIXIR-EE","publication":[{"doi":"10.1093/nar/gki372","metadata":{"abstract":"Nonsynonymous single nucleotide polymorphisms (nsSNPs) are prevalent in genomes and are closely associated with inherited diseases. To facilitate identifying disease-associated nsSNPs from a large number of neutral nsSNPs, it is important to develop computational tools to predict the nsSNP's phenotypic effect (disease-associated versus neutral). nsSNPAnalyzer, a web-based software developed for this purpose, extracts structural and evolutionary information from a query nsSNP and uses a machine learning method called Random Forest to predict the nsSNP's phenotypic effect. nsSNPAnalyzer server is available at http://snpanalyzer.utmem.edu/. \u00a9 2005 Oxford University Press.","authors":[{"name":"Bao L."},{"name":"Cui Y."},{"name":"Zhou M."}],"citationCount":180,"date":"2005-07-01